# OOP in Java - Lecture 6: Abstraction and Interfaces

These notes summarize the key ideas of abstraction and interfaces in Java. They are designed for classroom study and repository use, not a verbatim transcript of the lecture video.

## 1) What is abstraction?

Abstraction means hiding implementation details and showing only the essential features to the user.

For example:

- You know how to drive a car without knowing the engine internals.
- You use a `List` without caring how it is implemented internally.

In Java, abstraction is achieved through:

- abstract classes
- interfaces

## 2) Why abstraction is important

Abstraction helps us:

- reduce complexity
- focus on important behavior
- hide unnecessary details
- build flexible and scalable systems

It is one of the core principles of OOP.

## 3) Abstract classes

An abstract class cannot be instantiated directly. It is used as a base class for other classes.

```java
abstract class Animal {
    abstract void sound();
}
```

A subclass must implement all abstract methods unless it is also abstract.

```java
class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}
```

### Features of abstract classes

- can have abstract methods
- can have normal methods too
- can have instance variables
- can have constructors
- can be partially implemented

### When to use abstract classes

Use abstract classes when you want to:

- share common logic across several related classes
- define a common base with some default behavior
- require a common contract with partial implementation

## 4) Abstract methods

An abstract method has no body.

```java
abstract void draw();
```

The subclass provides the actual implementation.

This is useful when different subclasses need different behavior but share the same method name.

## 5) Interfaces

An interface is a completely abstract contract.

It defines what a class must do, not how it does it.

```java
interface Payment {
    void pay();
}
```

A class implements the interface:

```java
class UPI implements Payment {
    @Override
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("Pay using UPI");
    }
}
```

### Key points about interfaces

- all methods are abstract by default (before Java 8)
- a class can implement many interfaces
- interfaces support loose coupling
- they are used to define a standard behavior

## 6) Difference between abstract class and interface

| Feature | Abstract class | Interface |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Can have concrete methods | Yes | No (before Java 8) |
| Can have fields | Yes | Only constants |
| Multiple inheritance | No | Yes |
| Used for | Shared behavior and partial implementation | Contract and standard behavior |
| Instantiation | No | No |

### Simple rule

- Use an abstract class when classes are closely related and share common code.
- Use an interface when classes are unrelated but follow the same behavior.

## 7) Java 8+ interface features

Modern Java allows interfaces to include:

- default methods
- static methods

Example:

```java
interface Payment {
    void pay();

    default void printReceipt() {
        System.out.println("Receipt generated");
    }
}
```

This makes interfaces more powerful without forcing every class to reimplement the method.

## 8) Real-world examples

### Example 1: Vehicle hierarchy

```java
abstract class Vehicle {
    abstract void start();
}
```

`Car`, `Bike`, and `Truck` can extend `Vehicle` and implement `start()` differently.

### Example 2: Payment system

```java
interface Payment {
    void pay();
}
```

`CardPayment`, `UPIPayment`, and `CashPayment` can all implement `Payment` and behave differently while following the same contract.

## 9) Why interfaces are useful

Interfaces are very helpful when:

- multiple classes need the same method names
- you want to support multiple inheritance of behavior
- you want to reduce dependency on concrete classes
- you want a clean design for large projects

They are commonly used in:

- database APIs
- payment gateways
- sorting and comparison logic
- framework design

## 10) Polymorphism with abstraction and interfaces

Abstraction and interfaces work beautifully with polymorphism.

Example:

```java
Payment[] payments = {new UPI(), new Card()};

for (Payment payment : payments) {
    payment.pay();
}
```

Each object follows the same interface but performs different behavior.

## 11) Key takeaways

- Abstraction hides unnecessary implementation details.
- Abstract classes are used for shared code and common behavior.
- Abstract methods force subclasses to provide implementation.
- Interfaces define contracts.
- Classes can implement multiple interfaces.
- Interfaces help achieve loose coupling and polymorphism.

## 12) Interview-style summary

Be ready to explain:

- What is abstraction?
- Difference between abstract class and interface
- Why do we use abstract methods?
- When would you choose an interface over a class?
- How does abstraction support polymorphism?
- Can a class extend multiple classes? Can it implement multiple interfaces?

